{
  "dom_id": "step-q275538",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=\\sqrt{3},$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$",
  "worked_solution": "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
